## Persistent memory (graph-rag)

This project has an MCP-backed memory server. Use its tools to keep continuity
across sessions instead of re-deriving the same context every time:

- **`recall(query, top_k=5, kind=None, about_qualified_name=None)`** — at the
  start of a task, recall memories relevant to what you're about to do. It's a
  semantic search, so phrase `query` as a description of the task, not a
  keyword.
- **`remember(content, kind, about_qualified_name=None, importance=False)`**
  — save something worth keeping *as you learn it*, not at the end of the
  session as a summary. `kind` is one of:
  - `decision` — a choice made and why (architecture, library, approach).
  - `correction` — the user corrected your approach; record what and why, so
    it isn't repeated.
  - `finding` — something learned about the codebase that isn't obvious from
    reading it (a gotcha, a non-obvious invariant, a root cause).
  - `preference` — a standing preference about how to work in this project.
  - `fact` — a piece of project state that will go stale (a deadline, an
    owner, a decision pending elsewhere).
  Set `importance=True` for anything that should never be pruned by decay
  (`grag-mcp prune-memory`) even if it's never recalled again — e.g. a
  standing constraint, not a one-off finding.
- **`forget(memory_id)`** — delete a memory immediately if it turns out to be
  wrong or no longer applies. Don't leave stale memories for decay to
  eventually clean up if you already know they're wrong.

Tag a memory with `about_qualified_name=<dotted.path.to.Entity>` when it's
about one specific function/class/module — this makes it filterable later
even if the memory server runs separately from the code's own knowledge base.

Do **not** save: information already derivable from reading the code, git
history, or this file; ephemeral in-progress task state; anything that
belongs in a commit message or PR description instead.
